  {
    "episode_id": 90410,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90410.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "I suppose I didn't dwell on that personal aspect of it very much, partly because I knew that there was no choice from my perspective. I had to do what was the right thing at each moment, and I knew there'd be political consequences for that. I think that the extent to which people across Wyoming have been betrayed by Donald Trump is something that's very striking. I think you know you've certainly seen that around the country. We have very good hard-working people who have believed and continue to believe the lies and that's a really corrosive thing for our society. Do they feel you betrayed them? Yeah, I mean, look, and I think there are some people who have been betrayed by him and who I think he really is praying on people's patriotism. And I think that that's part of what's happening. But you also then have dangerous elements in our political culture today. And in Wyoming, you've got an oath keeper who's the chairman of our Republican state party, for example. And so I think that it's a combination of people who know better people who are enabling what's happening, people who've been betrayed. And then also, when you have groups like the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers and the 3%ers, who clearly were very much involved and engaged in leading the attack on the Capitol and the introduction of violence at that level. ways we've never seen before in terms of attempting to overturn an election. That is a very dangerous element and it's a really dangerous mix that characterizes our politics today.",
