  {
    "episode_id": 90410,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90410.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 66,
    "turn_text": "I look at it very much from the perspective of the impeachment, the duty and the authority, the obligation that goes with the impeachment power. And we learned lessons. I certainly learned lessons from the way that the Democrats handled that first impeachment. And I think that they would have been better served if they'd enforced some of their subpoenas. I think that when you're asked to take that step of voting for impeachment and it ultimately ended up being you know, a case that was based upon sort of second-hand pieces of information. And, you know, so I think based upon the case that was presented, I don't regret that vote. I certainly think, though, if you look at how we've conducted ourselves in the January 6th Committee and the extent to which we've really had I think an aggressive litigation strategy in a number of cases, and we've thought very carefully about making sure that we are taking steps that we need to take to provide all of the information. We also have in the January 6th committee had just the challenge has been how to ensure that we can organize and present the information because there's so much of it. But I think that's one of the most important differences between the two situations.",
