    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90410.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 112,
    "turn_text": "And maintain your office. And number one, they aren't going to prevail. You know, Donald Trump can say what he wants, but at the end of the day, I have total and complete confidence that there are many more Americans who are understand the threat that he poses and who are going to ensure that he's not successful and, you know, will ensure that the democracy and that the republic survives. So the story is not over yet, but it also is a case that the system doesn't work. The system breaks down very, very quickly if once people are elected, their sole purpose is maintaining their seat. There's a reason why we swear no to the Constitution. A reason why we all have to have a duty to something greater than political survival. It has made me think about term limits in a new way. I have opposed term limits and I think there are some constitutional difficulties with them and I think the voters ultimately ought to get to make those decisions. If you have members and I've had members say directly to me, you know, look, the coin is just political survival. Just keep your head down and survive. Well, you know, if the chips are down and the most dangerous moment for the nation that we've faced. ever from a president arrives and people don't do the right thing because they're just trying to hold on to office, then that makes it very difficult for the system to survive. But as I said, the country has been through crises, including, you know, devastating civil war. And at every moment of those crises, we have come out of it, we've come through it, and we've survived. And I'm confident, don't bet against America, don't bet against our democracy, don't bet against me.",
